{"content":[{"c":["AHIKAM"],"sourceClass":"h1","t":"p"},{"c":["Shaphan’s son, an officer of the court of King Josiah of Judah (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.22.12","r":"2Kgs.22.12","rawR":"2Kgs.22.12","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.22.12","value":"2Kgs.22.12"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 22:12"},"). Ahikam was among the group sent to the prophetess Huldah to ask about the Book of the Law (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.22.14-20","r":"2Kgs.22.14-20","rawR":"2Kgs.22.14-20","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.22.14-20","value":"2Kgs.22.14-20"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 22:14-20"},"). Later, under King Jehoiakim, Ahikam was able to prevent the prophet Jeremiah from being killed (",{"href":"?bref=Jer.26.24","r":"Jer.26.24","rawR":"Jer.26.24","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.26.24","value":"Jer.26.24"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 26:24"},"). Ahikam’s son Gedaliah was left as governor of Judah after Nebuchadnezzar destroyed Jerusalem and took most of its citizens to Babylon in 586 ",{"c":["BC"],"sourceClass":"era","t":"span"}," (",{"href":"?bref=2Kgs.25.22","r":"2Kgs.25.22","rawR":"2Kgs.25.22","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"2Kgs.25.22","value":"2Kgs.25.22"}],"t":"ref","v":"2 Kgs 25:22"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.39.14","r":"Jer.39.14","rawR":"Jer.39.14","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.39.14","value":"Jer.39.14"}],"t":"ref","v":"Jer 39:14"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.40.5-16","r":"Jer.40.5-16","rawR":"Jer.40.5-16","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.40.5-16","value":"Jer.40.5-16"}],"t":"ref","v":"40:5-16"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.41.1-18","r":"Jer.41.1-18","rawR":"Jer.41.1-18","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.41.1-18","value":"Jer.41.1-18"}],"t":"ref","v":"41:1-18"},"; ",{"href":"?bref=Jer.43.6","r":"Jer.43.6","rawR":"Jer.43.6","sourceRefs":[{"raw":"Jer.43.6","value":"Jer.43.6"}],"t":"ref","v":"43:6"},")."],"sourceClass":"fl","t":"p"}],"format":"bsb-study-dictionary-v2","id":"Ahikam","provenance":{"archiveSha256":"10758ea00dbea7540f9337e43e59c8e4000295c1261e3426bab6f31c31dcff5b","release":"1.6","sourceCitationReferences":[{"anchorText":"2 Kgs 22:12","canonicalizat